PR #717  overall: compliant
Spec: CLIC xivt CSR field width documented as XLEN-6 (instead of XLEN-2) causes upper 4 address bits to be dropped, misdirecting interrupt handler dispatch to wrong memory locations
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:319-540  (global CSR constant definitions)
      No CLIC xivt/xeivt CSR constants or bit-field extraction macros exist in this tree. CSR_STVEC (0x105), CSR_MTVEC (0x305), and CSR_VSTVEC (0x205) are defined as CSR numbers only — no address-base GENMASK macros are applied to them. CSR_TVEC is conditionally #define'd to CSR_MTVEC or CSR_STVEC and written as a full register value (e.g., csrw CSR_TVEC, a0) at all call sites. Because there is no xivt/xeivt support at all, the XLEN-6 vs XLEN-2 width bug cannot manifest. The existing GENMASK usage in this file (GENMASK(11,0), GENMASK(27,16), etc.) uses explicit bit indices and is correct for the non-CLIC CSRs it describes.
  [OK] drivers/irqchip/irq-riscv-intc.c:29-208  riscv_intc_init_common / riscv_intc_irq / riscv_intc_aia_irq
      The driver initialises IRQ dispatch via set_handle_irq() to either riscv_intc_irq (standard xtvec cause-based dispatch) or riscv_intc_aia_irq (AIA TOPI-based dispatch). No CLIC code path exists — the driver never reads, writes, or extracts an address field from xivt/xeivt. riscv_intc_irq extracts the interrupt cause from the scause CSR (regs->cause & ~CAUSE_IRQ_FLAG), which is independent of the vector table base. The only CSR reads/writes are to SIE/SIEH for masking, not to any vector-table CSR. No address truncation can occur here because no vector-table CSR address field is being extracted.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:74-213  _start / relocate_enable_mmu / .Lsetup_trap_vector
      All CSR_TVEC writes use full virtual addresses (la + csrw CSR_TVEC, aN), e.g., 'la a0, handle_exception; csrw CSR_TVEC, a0' at line 205-206. The values written are 4-byte aligned by virtue of being code labels (RISC-V instructions are aligned). For standard xtvec (non-CLIC), the bottom 2 bits encode the mode: MODE=0 means 'direct' (all traps go to BASE), which is what the kernel uses — the address naturally has bits [1:0]=0, so the write implicitly selects direct mode. No bit-field extraction from a CSR value is performed. There is no CLIC-mode path that would write to xivt/xeivt. Correct and safe for the pre-CLIC baseline.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:125-318  handle_exception / excp_vect_table
      handle_exception is the single trap entry point installed via CSR_TVEC (xtvec direct mode). It dispatches interrupts via 'bge s4, zero, 1f; call do_irq' based on the MSB of scause, and exceptions via a software jump table (excp_vect_table) indexed by the lower bits of scause. This dispatch is entirely scause-driven — no CSR address-field extraction occurs. The excp_vect_table is defined at ELF level (linker-allocated) and guaranteed aligned. On CLIC hardware, hardware vectored mode (xivt/xeivt) would use a per-interrupt-ID hardware vector table, but this code does not implement that. Correct for the pre-CLIC state.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:438-459  do_irq / handle_riscv_irq
      do_irq → handle_riscv_irq → handle_arch_irq(regs) is a pure function-pointer dispatch chain. No CSR is read, written, or bit-field-extracted in this path. handle_arch_irq was set by riscv_intc_init_common() to either riscv_intc_irq or riscv_intc_aia_irq, neither of which touches the vector-table CSR. The interrupt vector table base address is never accessed here — this is purely downstream dispatch. No address truncation can occur.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_switch.S:41-161  SAVE_HOST_AND_RESTORE_GUEST_CSRS / SAVE_GUEST_AND_RESTORE_HOST_CSRS
      The VM-entry macro SAVE_HOST_AND_RESTORE_GUEST_CSRS saves host CSR_STVEC and restores the guest trap-return path into CSR_STVEC. The VM-exit macro SAVE_GUEST_AND_RESTORE_HOST_CSRS restores host CSR_STVEC. Critically, these macros only handle CSR_STVEC (0x105) — the host physical stvec. On CLIC hardware, the host's CLIC interrupt vector table base is held in xeivt (a separate CSR), not stvec. The code assumes CSR_STVEC is the sole trap-vector CSR, which is true for the baseline xtvec-only architecture but implicitly relies on CLIC not being active. If a future CLIC patchset adds xeivt save/restore without updating these macros, guest interrupts will use a stale xeivt base across VM boundaries.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:578-683  kvm_arch_vcpu_load / kvm_arch_vcpu_put
      kvm_arch_vcpu_load writes csr->vstvec to CSR_VSTVEC (the guest's virtualised stvec, 0x205). kvm_arch_vcpu_put reads CSR_VSTVEC back. The full unsigned long is written/read — no bit-field masking or address extraction is performed. This is correct for the pre-CLIC state where VSTVEC fully captures the guest trap vector. On CLIC hardware, the guest's CLIC vector table base would be held in vsxivt/vsxeivt (hypervisor-context CLIC CSRs), not vstvec. The code implicitly assumes VSTVEC is the sole trap-vector state. When CLIC guest support is added, vsxivt/vsxeivt CSR save/restore would need to be added here, and any GENMASK-based address extraction from those CSRs would be the point where an XLEN-6 vs XLEN-2 bug could manifest.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/hwcap.h:13-128  (ISA extension bitmap definitions)
