PR #717  overall: potential_risk
Spec: CLIC xivt CSR field width documented as XLEN-6 (instead of XLEN-2) causes upper 4 address bits to be dropped, misdirecting interrupt handler dispatch to wrong memory locations
  [RISK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:89-89  (top-level constant)
      MTVEC_MODE is defined as _UL(0x00000003), a 2-bit mask correct for standard RISC-V tvec CSRs (where MODE occupies bits [1:0]). However, this constant is used in at least two call sites (sbi_trap.c:172 and :207) to mask CSR_VSTVEC and CSR_STVEC. If CLIC xivt/xeivt CSRs were introduced, reusing this 2-bit constant would truncate the upper 4 bits of the 6-bit CLIC MODE field, causing the computed handler address to land in an unintended memory region. The constant itself is technically correct for current usage but its name invites misuse in CLIC contexts.
  [OK] firmware/fw_base.S:545-577  TRAP_SAVE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_SP_T0 (macro)
      The macro saves a6 at line 561 alongside all other GPRs. The corresponding TRAP_RESTORE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_A0_T0 macro restores a6 at line 629. Save and restore are symmetric — both handle a6. The push/pop mismatch vulnerability described in the PR context (a6 reportedly removed from the save side but still loaded on return) does NOT exist in the current code. The a6 save at line 561 and restore at line 629 are in lockstep.
  [OK] firmware/fw_base.S:614-645  TRAP_RESTORE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_A0_T0 (macro)
      Restores a6 at line 629 from the stack slot written by TRAP_SAVE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_SP_T0 at line 561. Both macros are invoked in the same trap handler flow (_trap_handler at line 678/684, _trap_handler_hyp at line 704/714), so the save/restore pairing is always maintained. No mismatch exists in the current code.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-fast-interrupt/pr-717/

  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:172-172  sbi_trap_redirect
      Extracts the VS-mode trap handler base address via 'csr_read(CSR_VSTVEC) & ~MTVEC_MODE', using the 2-bit MTVEC_MODE mask. For standard RISC-V vstvec this is correct — MODE is bits [1:0]. However, if VS-level CLIC xivt/xeivt CSRs were introduced and this same pattern (or the same MTVEC_MODE constant) were applied to them, the 6-bit CLIC MODE field would be incompletely cleared, corrupting the upper 4 bits of the computed base address. The current code is correct but the invariant 'only standard tvec CSRs, never CLIC IVT CSRs' is not enforced locally.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:207-207  sbi_trap_redirect
      Same pattern as line 172 but for S-mode: 'csr_read(CSR_STVEC) & ~MTVEC_MODE'. Correct for standard stvec (2-bit MODE field). Would produce a silently-wrong address if applied to a CLIC xivt/xeivt CSR without widening the mask to 6 bits (0x3F). The risk is latent — no CLIC CSR reads exist in the current codebase, but there is no guard preventing future misuse of the 2-bit constant.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:810-828  sbi_hart_switch_mode
      Writes next_addr directly into CSR_VSTVEC (line 812), CSR_STVEC (line 817), and CSR_UTVEC (line 824) without setting any MODE bits. For standard RISC-V tvec, MODE=0 selects 'direct' mode where all traps jump to the address in BASE. This is correct — next_addr is the trap handler address, and direct mode is appropriate for switching to a new privilege mode's handler. If CLIC xivt/xeivt CSR writes were added here, a 6-bit CLIC mode encoding would be needed, but that is a future concern.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:130-140  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Saves/restores stvec as an opaque full-width CSR value via csr_swap (line 133). The entire register — including MODE bits, BASE address, and any reserved fields — is atomically exchanged and stored in the domain context structure. This is safe: it preserves whatever MODE encoding was active, whether standard tvec (2-bit) or future CLIC (6-bit). Downstream code that interprets the saved value with a 2-bit mask is a separate concern, not a defect here.
  [OK] firmware/fw_base.S:394-401  _start_warm
      Writes the trap handler address directly into CSR_MTVEC via 'csrw CSR_MTVEC, a4' at line 401. For standard mtvec, writing the address with bit [1:0] = 0 selects direct mode — all traps vector to the BASE address. This is correct for boot-time initialization. If CLIC initialization required writing xivt/xeivt CSRs with a 6-bit MODE field, additional writes would be needed, but the current mtvec write is a correct and necessary baseline setup.
  [OK] include/sbi/sbi_bitops.h:19-22  (top-level macro)
      EXTRACT_FIELD and INSERT_FIELD are generic bit-manipulation macros. Their correctness depends entirely on the mask constant passed as the 'which' parameter. The macros themselves correctly compute field values: EXTRACT_FIELD isolates and right-shifts; INSERT_FIELD clears then places. No intrinsic width bug exists. The risk is purely at call sites that might pass an incorrect mask — that is a caller defect, not a macro defect.

